The statutory rule is:

- When the Superintendent of Surveys is satisfied that the condominium plan conforms to the Apartment Ownership Law, No. 11 of 1973 and this Act, he shall certify the plan on behalf of the Surveyor-General and then:
  - file the original of the condominium plan after making a suitable reference on the cadastral map used for registration of title; and
  - forward the duplicate of the condominium plan and the owner’s application to the appropriate Registrar of Title.

- When the Registrar of Title is satisfied that the application conforms to the Apartment Ownership Law, No. 11 of 1973 and this Act, he shall:
  - file the duplicate of the condominium plan in the Parcels File;
  - open a new title register in respect of each condominium parcel shown on the Condominium Plan and make suitable cross references in the register in which the land parcel is registered;
  - record in the Property Section that the land comprised therein consists only of common property;
  - record in the Encumbrances Section that registers in respect of condominium parcels have been opened;
  - inform the applicant that the condominium parcels have been registered and notify him of the reference numbers of the relevant registers; and
  - issue a Certificate of Title after recovery of the prescribed fees.

Applied here, because the condominium plan has been approved as conforming to the applicable laws, the Superintendent must certify it, file the original with the suitable cadastral-map reference, and forward the duplicate and the owner’s application to the Registrar. The Registrar must then, if satisfied that the application conforms, complete the registration steps listed above for the condominium parcels.
